window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"Claude & Claude Code — a visual field guide (June 2026)","section":"Learn Claude & Claude Code — the model and the tool.","tool":"home","text":"Learn Claude & Claude Code — the model and the tool. A visual, beginner-first guide in two tracks. Using Claude teaches how the models think and how to talk to them — prompting, the API, tool use. Claude Code teaches the agentic coding tool you'll use every day — the explore→plan→code→commit workflo","keywords":["learn","claude","code","the","model","and","tool"]},{"url":"claude/what-is-claude.html","page":"What is Claude (and an LLM)? — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"What is Claude (and an LLM)?","tool":"claude","text":"What is Claude (and an LLM)? Claude is Anthropic's family of large language models (LLMs) — AI systems trained to read text and produce a helpful, fluent response. It's the “brain” you met in Track B: the thing Claude Code calls to decide what to do. Here we look at the brain itself.","keywords":["what","is","claude","and","an","llm"]},{"url":"claude/what-is-claude.html#llm","page":"What is Claude (and an LLM)? — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"What an LLM actually does","tool":"claude","text":"01 What an LLM actually does Under the hood, an LLM does one deceptively simple thing: given some text, it predicts what comes next, one chunk (a “token”) at a time. Do that prediction extremely well, having read a huge slice of human writing and code, and something remarkable emerges — it can answe","keywords":["what","an","llm","actually","does"]},{"url":"claude/what-is-claude.html#claude","page":"What is Claude (and an LLM)? — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"What makes Claude, Claude","tool":"claude","text":"02 What makes Claude, Claude Claude is built by Anthropic with a strong emphasis on being helpful, honest, and harmless . In practice you'll notice it's good at careful reasoning, following nuanced instructions, long-context work, and writing & reviewing code — which is exactly why it powers Claude ","keywords":["what","makes","claude"]},{"url":"claude/what-is-claude.html#limits","page":"What is Claude (and an LLM)? — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Two honest limits","tool":"claude","text":"03 Two honest limits It can be confidently wrong gotcha Because it predicts plausible text, Claude can state something false as if it's sure (a “hallucination”). For anything that matters, give it sources, ask it to show its work, and verify — the same instinct as closing the loop in Claude Code. It","keywords":["two","honest","limits"]},{"url":"claude/models.html","page":"The model family — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"The model family","tool":"claude","text":"The model family Claude comes in three sizes — Opus , Sonnet , and Haiku — named like a progression from grandest to shortest. They share the same skills; they trade capability against speed and cost . Picking the right one for the job is most of “using Claude well.”","keywords":["the","model","family"]},{"url":"claude/models.html#three","page":"The model family — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Three sizes","tool":"claude","text":"01 Three sizes Opus The most capable. Deep reasoning, long-horizon agentic coding, the hardest problems. Slower and priciest — bring it out when the task is genuinely hard. Sonnet The balanced default. Excellent intelligence at much better speed and cost. The workhorse for most coding and app work. ","keywords":["three","sizes"]},{"url":"claude/models.html#ids","page":"The model family — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"The lineup (June 2026)","tool":"claude","text":"02 The lineup (June 2026) Model Model ID Context Reach for it when… Claude Opus 4.8 claude-opus-4-8 1M tokens Complex reasoning, long agentic coding sessions, high-autonomy work Claude Sonnet 4.6 claude-sonnet-4-6 1M tokens Most day-to-day building — the best speed/intelligence balance Claude Haiku ","keywords":["the","lineup","june","model","opus","sonnet","haiku"]},{"url":"claude/models.html#window","page":"The model family — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Context windows & thinking","tool":"claude","text":"03 Context windows & thinking The context window is how much text a model can consider at once (the same idea as context in Claude Code ). A 1M-token window can hold an entire large codebase or a stack of documents in one go. Most current models can also do extended thinking — spend extra steps reas","keywords":["context","windows","thinking","effort"]},{"url":"claude/talking-to-claude.html","page":"Talking to Claude — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Talking to Claude","tool":"claude","text":"Talking to Claude There are three front doors to Claude: the chat app for everyday questions, the Console for experimenting and getting API keys, and the API for building it into software. They all speak the same underlying language — a conversation of messages with roles.","keywords":["talking","to","claude"]},{"url":"claude/talking-to-claude.html#doors","page":"Talking to Claude — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Three ways in","tool":"claude","text":"01 Three ways in Chat · claude.ai The friendly web/app chat. Best for questions, drafting, and exploring — no setup. everyday Console / Workbench console.anthropic.com — try prompts, tune system prompts, and get your API key. build & test The API & SDK Call Claude from your own code (Python, TS, mor","keywords":["three","ways","in"]},{"url":"claude/talking-to-claude.html#messages","page":"Talking to Claude — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"A conversation is a list of messages","tool":"claude","text":"02 A conversation is a list of messages Whichever door you use, a conversation is a sequence of messages , each with a role . There are three: system The setup: who Claude should be and the rules to follow (“You are a terse SQL expert. Only return queries.”). Set once; it shapes the whole conversati","keywords":["conversation","is","list","of","messages"]},{"url":"claude/talking-to-claude.html#stateless","page":"Talking to Claude — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"The model has no memory between calls","tool":"claude","text":"03 The model has no memory between calls This surprises people: Claude doesn't remember your last conversation. Each request is stateless — the model only knows what's in the messages you send this time . The chat app feels like it remembers because it quietly resends the history every turn. When yo","keywords":["the","model","has","no","memory","between","calls"]},{"url":"claude/prompting-basics.html","page":"Prompting 1 · be clear — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Prompting 1 · be clear & direct","tool":"claude","text":"Prompting 1 · be clear & direct A prompt is just your instructions to Claude — and the quality of the answer tracks the quality of the ask. The number-one rule isn't a trick: be clear and direct. Treat Claude like a sharp new colleague on their first day, who has zero context about your situation un","keywords":["prompting","be","clear","direct"]},{"url":"claude/prompting-basics.html#three","page":"Prompting 1 · be clear — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Three things a good prompt has","tool":"claude","text":"01 Three things a good prompt has Context Who's it for, what's the situation, what background does Claude need? Don't make it guess. A clear task One specific thing to do, in plain language. “Summarize,” “rewrite,” “find the bug in,” “draft.” The shape of “done” Format and constraints: how long, wha","keywords":["three","things","good","prompt","has"]},{"url":"claude/prompting-basics.html#lab","page":"Prompting 1 · be clear — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Feel the difference","tool":"claude","text":"02 Feel the difference Flip between the two prompts below and press Ask Claude . Same task, two levels of clarity — watch the reply (and the “why”) change. You can edit the prompt and ask again.","keywords":["feel","the","difference"]},{"url":"claude/prompting-basics.html#habits","page":"Prompting 1 · be clear — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Quick wins","tool":"claude","text":"03 Quick wins Show the format Ask for bullets, a table, JSON, or “just the code.” If output shape matters, say it. State constraints Length, tone, audience, what to avoid. “Under 100 words, no marketing fluff.” Give the material Paste the text, the error, the data. Claude can't see what you don't in","keywords":["quick","wins"]},{"url":"claude/prompting-advanced.html","page":"Prompting 2 · examples & thinking — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Prompting 2 · examples & thinking","tool":"claude","text":"Prompting 2 · examples & thinking Clarity gets you 80% of the way. These four techniques get the rest: show examples , let Claude think , structure with XML tags , and set a strong system prompt . They're Anthropic's own headline recommendations — and each is easy to apply.","keywords":["prompting","examples","thinking"]},{"url":"claude/prompting-advanced.html#examples","page":"Prompting 2 · examples & thinking — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Show, don't just tell (few-shot)","tool":"claude","text":"01 Show, don't just tell (few-shot) The single most powerful upgrade: include a few examples of the input → output you want. Examples pin down the format and the edge cases far better than describing them. Try it — the “no examples” reply is fine but unpredictable; the “few-shot” reply is clean and ","keywords":["show","don","just","tell","few","shot"]},{"url":"claude/prompting-advanced.html#think","page":"Prompting 2 · examples & thinking — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Let Claude think first","tool":"claude","text":"02 Let Claude think first For anything involving reasoning — math, logic, multi-step decisions — telling Claude to think step by step before answering measurably improves accuracy. It works because the model reasons in the tokens it generates; give it room to work and it stumbles less. prompt Copy W","keywords":["let","claude","think","first","Work","through","this","step","by","inside","thinking","tags","then","give","your","final","answer","in","train","leaves","at","pm","going","mph","effort"]},{"url":"claude/prompting-advanced.html#xml","page":"Prompting 2 · examples & thinking — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Structure inputs with XML tags","tool":"claude","text":"03 Structure inputs with XML tags When a prompt mixes instructions with data (a document to analyze, examples, context), wrap each part in named XML tags . Claude is specifically tuned to respect them, so it never confuses your instructions with the material. prompt Copy Summarize the document for a","keywords":["structure","inputs","with","xml","tags","Summarize","the","document","for","new","hire","Use","only","what","in","paste","long","doc","here","format","short","bullets","plain","English","answer"]},{"url":"claude/prompting-advanced.html#system","page":"Prompting 2 · examples & thinking — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Set the role in the system prompt","tool":"claude","text":"04 Set the role in the system prompt Put durable instructions — who Claude is, the rules, the tone — in the system prompt (from the last lesson ). It anchors every turn, so you don't repeat yourself. system prompt Copy You are a senior code reviewer for a Python team. Be concise and specific. Cite l","keywords":["set","the","role","in","system","prompt","You","are","senior","code","reviewer","for","Python","team","Be","concise","and","specific","Cite","line","numbers","Prefer","small","safe","diffs","If","something","is","unclear","ask","before","assuming","Never","invent","APIs"]},{"url":"claude/api-basics.html","page":"The API & SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"The API & SDK","tool":"claude","text":"The API & SDK To build Claude into your own software, you call the Messages API — usually through an official SDK. It's the same conversation you met in the chat (system + user + assistant messages), now as a function call. Here's your first call, multi-turn, and streaming — copy and run them.","keywords":["the","api","sdk"]},{"url":"claude/api-basics.html#setup","page":"The API & SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Install & authenticate","tool":"claude","text":"01 Install & authenticate Grab an API key from the Console (console.anthropic.com → API keys), set it as an environment variable, and install the SDK. Python TypeScript Copy pip install anthropic export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=\"sk-ant-...\" npm install @anthropic-ai/sdk export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=\"sk-ant-...","keywords":["install","authenticate","pip","anthropic","export","ANTHROPIC_API_KEY","sk","ant","npm","ai","sdk"]},{"url":"claude/api-basics.html#first","page":"The API & SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Your first call","tool":"claude","text":"02 Your first call Three required pieces: a model , a max_tokens ceiling, and a list of messages . The reply comes back in content . Python TypeScript Copy from anthropic import Anthropic client = Anthropic() # reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY msg = client.messages.create( model=\"claude-sonnet-4-6\", max_toke","keywords":["your","first","call","model","max_tokens","messages","content","from","anthropic","import","Anthropic","client","reads","ANTHROPIC_API_KEY","msg","create","claude","sonnet","system","You","are","concise","assistant","role","user","Give","me","names","for","coffee","app","print","text","ai","sdk","const","new","await","console","log"]},{"url":"claude/api-basics.html#multiturn","page":"The API & SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Multi-turn: you hold the history","tool":"claude","text":"03 Multi-turn: you hold the history Remember, the model is stateless. To continue a conversation, append Claude's reply and your next message to the list, then call again with the full history. Python TypeScript Copy history = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Suggest a name for a coffee app.\"}] r1 = cl","keywords":["multi","turn","you","hold","the","history","role","user","content","Suggest","name","for","coffee","app","r1","client","messages","create","model","claude","sonnet","max_tokens","512","append","Claude","reply","then","your","follow","up","assistant","text","Make","it","more","playful","r2","print","const","await"]},{"url":"claude/api-basics.html#stream","page":"The API & SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Streaming for snappy UIs","tool":"claude","text":"04 Streaming for snappy UIs For chat interfaces, stream the response so text appears token-by-token instead of after a pause. Python TypeScript Copy with client.messages.stream( model=\"claude-sonnet-4-6\", max_tokens=1024, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Write a haiku about coffee.\"}], ) as st","keywords":["streaming","for","snappy","uis","with","client","messages","stream","model","claude","sonnet","max_tokens","role","user","content","Write","haiku","about","coffee","as","text","in","text_stream","print","end","flush","True","const","await","event","of","if","type","content_block_delta","delta","text_delta","process","stdout","write"]},{"url":"claude/tool-use.html","page":"Tool use (function calling) — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Tool use (function calling)","tool":"claude","text":"Tool use (function calling) On its own, Claude only produces text. Give it tools — functions you describe — and it can decide to call them: look up live data, query a database, send an email. You run the function and hand back the result. This request → call → result → answer loop is exactly how age","keywords":["tool","use","function","calling"]},{"url":"claude/tool-use.html#loop","page":"Tool use (function calling) — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"The loop","tool":"claude","text":"01 The loop One tool-use round-trip 1 you send prompt + tool defs 2 Claude asks “call get_weather(NYC)” 3 you run it return “12°C” 4 Claude answers “It's 12°C in NYC.” Crucial: Claude never runs the tool — it only asks. Your code runs it and returns the result. That's the safety boundary, and the sa","keywords":["the","loop"]},{"url":"claude/tool-use.html#define","page":"Tool use (function calling) — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Define a tool","tool":"claude","text":"02 Define a tool A tool is a name, a description, and a JSON-schema for its inputs. The description matters — it's how Claude knows when to reach for it. tools.py Copy tools = [{ \"name\": \"get_weather\", \"description\": \"Get the current temperature for a city.\", \"input_schema\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"pro","keywords":["define","tool","tools","name","get_weather","description","Get","the","current","temperature","for","city","input_schema","type","object","properties","string","City","required"]},{"url":"claude/tool-use.html#run","page":"Tool use (function calling) — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Run the loop","tool":"claude","text":"03 Run the loop Send the tools. If Claude wants one, the response's stop_reason is \"tool_use\" . Run the function, append the result as a tool_result , and call again so Claude can finish. app.py Copy def get_weather(city): # your real implementation return \"12°C and clear\" messages = [{\"role\": \"user","keywords":["run","the","loop","stop_reason","tool_use","tool_result","def","get_weather","city","your","real","implementation","return","and","clear","messages","role","user","content","What","weather","in","NYC","resp","client","create","model","claude","sonnet","max_tokens","tools","if","call","next","for","type","result","input","tool","yourself"]},{"url":"claude/tool-use.html#agents","page":"Tool use (function calling) — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Loop it, and you have an agent","tool":"claude","text":"04 Loop it, and you have an agent Run that round-trip in a while loop — keep going as long as Claude keeps asking for tools, stop when it returns a final answer — and you've built a tiny agent. Add tools like read file , edit file , and run command , and you've got the skeleton of Claude Code itself","keywords":["loop","it","and","you","have","an","agent","while"]},{"url":"claude/power-features.html","page":"Power features — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Power features","tool":"claude","text":"Power features Once your app works, these features make it faster, cheaper, and more capable . You don't need them on day one — but knowing they exist changes what you'll build. Here's the tour, with the “reach for it when” for each.","keywords":["power","features"]},{"url":"claude/power-features.html#caching","page":"Power features — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Prompt caching — the big cost lever","tool":"claude","text":"01 Prompt caching — the big cost lever If you send the same long prefix every call — a big system prompt, a document, a set of examples — cache it. Cached input is dramatically cheaper and faster on subsequent calls. You mark a cache breakpoint and Claude reuses everything up to it. caching.py Copy ","keywords":["prompt","caching","the","big","cost","lever","client","messages","create","model","claude","sonnet","max_tokens","512","system","type","text","LONG_KNOWLEDGE_BASE","reused","every","call","cache_control","ephemeral","cache","breakpoint","role","user","content","Answer","from","KB","above"]},{"url":"claude/power-features.html#more","page":"Power features — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"The rest of the toolkit","tool":"claude","text":"02 The rest of the toolkit Extended thinking Let Claude reason for longer before answering — big accuracy gains on math, logic, and planning. Set a thinking budget; in Claude Code it's the /effort slider. Vision & PDF Send images and PDFs as input. Claude reads charts, screenshots, scanned docs — gr","keywords":["the","rest","of","toolkit","effort"]},{"url":"claude/power-features.html#choose","page":"Power features — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Which one, when","tool":"claude","text":"03 Which one, when Your goal Reach for Cut cost & latency on repeated context Prompt caching Harder reasoning / fewer mistakes Extended thinking Analyze images, charts, or PDFs Vision / PDF Process a huge backlog cheaply Batch API Output must be valid JSON for code Structured outputs Answers must be","keywords":["which","one","when"]},{"url":"claude/agents-and-mcp.html","page":"Agents, MCP & the Agent SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Agents, MCP & the Agent SDK","tool":"claude","text":"Agents, MCP & the Agent SDK You now have every ingredient of an agent : a model, prompts, tool use, and a loop. This lesson zooms out — what makes a good agent, how MCP connects it to the world, and the Claude Agent SDK that hands you the whole machine. It's also the bridge: the SDK is exactly what ","keywords":["agents","mcp","the","agent","sdk"]},{"url":"claude/agents-and-mcp.html#what","page":"Agents, MCP & the Agent SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"What an agent is","tool":"claude","text":"01 What an agent is An agent is an LLM running in a loop with tools: it takes a goal, decides an action, runs it, observes the result, and repeats until done. You saw the bones in Tool use ; you saw the polished version in Claude Code's agent loop . Same idea, both times. The agent loop (sound famil","keywords":["what","an","agent","is"]},{"url":"claude/agents-and-mcp.html#effective","page":"Agents, MCP & the Agent SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"Anthropic's advice: keep it simple","tool":"claude","text":"02 Anthropic's advice: keep it simple From Anthropic's Building Effective Agents guidance, the recurring theme is restraint: Start simple Many “agents” are a few lines around the API. Reach for one capable agent with good tools before you build a multi-agent system. Design tools like a human's Each ","keywords":["anthropic","advice","keep","it","simple"]},{"url":"claude/agents-and-mcp.html#mcp","page":"Agents, MCP & the Agent SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"MCP: tools without the glue code","tool":"claude","text":"03 MCP: tools without the glue code You met MCP in Claude Code — it's a platform standard, not a Claude Code feature. Instead of hand-writing a tool for every external system, you connect an MCP server and your agent gets that system's tools for free. Write the adapter once; every MCP-aware agent ca","keywords":["mcp","tools","without","the","glue","code"]},{"url":"claude/agents-and-mcp.html#sdk","page":"Agents, MCP & the Agent SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"The Claude Agent SDK","tool":"claude","text":"04 The Claude Agent SDK Rather than hand-roll the loop, permissions, and context management, use the Claude Agent SDK (Python & TypeScript). It packages everything you've learned — the agent loop, tool use, MCP, subagents, hooks — into a library. It's the same engine that powers Claude Code. agent.p","keywords":["the","claude","agent","sdk","pip","install","an","that","can","read","edit","files","and","run","commands","in","few","lines","import","anyio","from","claude_agent_sdk","query","async","def","main","for","message","prompt","Add","health","route","test","then","tests","print"]},{"url":"claude/agents-and-mcp.html#wrap","page":"Agents, MCP & the Agent SDK — Using Claude · Field Guide","section":"You've finished the guide 🎉","tool":"claude","text":"05 You've finished the guide 🎉 That's the whole arc: from what an LLM is , through prompting and the API, to building agents — and across in Track B, the agent you'll use every day. The two tracks are one story: Claude is the brain; Claude Code is the brain with hands. Keep the cheat sheet handy, s","keywords":["you","ve","finished","the","guide"]},{"url":"code/what-is-claude-code.html","page":"What is Claude Code? — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"What is Claude Code?","tool":"code","text":"What is Claude Code? Claude Code is an agentic coding tool : it reads your codebase, edits files, runs commands, and uses your dev tools — working across many files while you watch, redirect, or step away. It's the model (Claude) wrapped in a body that can actually touch your project.","keywords":["what","is","claude","code"]},{"url":"code/what-is-claude-code.html#not-a-chatbot","page":"What is Claude Code? — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Not a chatbot — an agent","tool":"code","text":"01 Not a chatbot — an agent A chatbot answers your question and waits. Claude Code does the work : it explores, plans, edits, runs tests, and reports back. You give it a goal in plain English; it takes real actions to reach it. A chat assistant Claude Code You copy code in, paste answers out It read","keywords":["not","chatbot","an","agent"]},{"url":"code/what-is-claude-code.html#surfaces","page":"What is Claude Code? — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Five surfaces, one engine","tool":"code","text":"02 Five surfaces, one engine The same Claude Code runs in five places. Your CLAUDE.md , settings, MCP servers, and skills work the same everywhere — pick whichever fits the moment. Terminal CLI The most feature-rich surface. Just type claude in any project. power users VS Code & JetBrains A graphica","keywords":["five","surfaces","one","engine","CLAUDE","md","claude"]},{"url":"code/what-is-claude-code.html#install","page":"What is Claude Code? — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Install & first run","tool":"code","text":"03 Install & first run One command installs it; claude starts it. Native installs auto-update in the background. terminal Copy # macOS / Linux / WSL curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/install.sh | bash # Windows (PowerShell) # irm https://claude.ai/install.ps1 | iex # start it in your project cd my-projec","keywords":["install","first","run","claude","macOS","Linux","WSL","curl","fsSL","https","ai","sh","bash","Windows","PowerShell","irm","ps1","iex","start","it","in","your","project","cd","my","init","CLAUDE","md"]},{"url":"code/what-is-claude-code.html#when","page":"What is Claude Code? — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"What it's great at","tool":"code","text":"04 What it's great at Shipping changes Features, bug fixes, refactors, tests — the everyday loop of writing and changing code across files. Understanding code “How does auth work here?” “Where's rate limiting?” It reads the repo and explains, no edits needed. Tedious-but-careful work Migrations, ren","keywords":["what","it","great","at"]},{"url":"code/first-session.html","page":"Your first session — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Your first session","tool":"code","text":"Your first session Open a terminal in a project, type claude , and ask for something in plain English. From there, Claude Code runs a simple loop over and over: gather context → act → verify → repeat . Once you can see that loop, everything else in this track is just a detail of it.","keywords":["your","first","session"]},{"url":"code/first-session.html#start","page":"Your first session — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Start it in a project","tool":"code","text":"01 Start it in a project Install once, then run claude from inside any project folder. It starts an interactive session in your terminal — a conversation that can also read files, edit them, and run commands. terminal Copy # install (macOS / Linux / WSL) — one line curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/insta","keywords":["start","it","in","project","claude","install","macOS","Linux","WSL","one","line","curl","fsSL","https","ai","sh","bash","then","inside","your","cd","my","irm","ps1","iex"]},{"url":"code/first-session.html#loop","page":"Your first session — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"The loop in one picture","tool":"code","text":"02 The loop in one picture Claude Code is not a chatbot that answers and waits. It's an agent : given a goal, it repeats a short cycle until the goal is met (or it needs you). The agent loop 1 · gather read context files, grep, output 2 · act use a tool edit, run, write 3 · verify check result tests","keywords":["the","loop","in","one","picture"]},{"url":"code/first-session.html#watch","page":"Your first session — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Watch a first session","tool":"code","text":"03 Watch a first session Here's a real-shaped session. Press play — or step with ‹ › — and read the caption under each step. Notice how it reads before it writes, and runs the script after it edits. claude — ~/notes-cli default An animated, step-through Claude Code session. Enable JavaScript to play","keywords":["watch","first","session"]},{"url":"code/first-session.html#talk","page":"Your first session — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"How to talk to it","tool":"code","text":"04 How to talk to it You don't need special syntax — just clear requests. A few habits make a big difference from day one: Say what “done” looks like “Add input validation” is vague. “Reject empty names with a 400 and a clear message; add a test” gives Claude a target it can hit and check. Give it a","keywords":["how","to","talk","it","foo"]},{"url":"code/first-session.html#read","page":"Your first session — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Reading the screen","tool":"code","text":"05 Reading the screen During a session you'll see a few recurring things. Now that you've watched one, they'll be familiar: You see… It means… A “thinking” line Claude is reasoning about the next step (you can expand it to read along). A tool call (Read / Edit / Bash …) The model asked to do somethi","keywords":["reading","the","screen"]},{"url":"code/context.html","page":"Context is currency — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Context is currency","tool":"code","text":"Context is currency The context window is everything Claude can “see” right now — your prompts, the files it read, command output, its own replies. It's the model's working memory, and it's finite. Spend it on what matters and sessions stay sharp; fill it with noise and quality quietly drops. Managi","keywords":["context","is","currency"]},{"url":"code/context.html#what","page":"Context is currency — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Think of it as RAM","tool":"code","text":"01 Think of it as RAM Modern Claude models have huge context windows (Opus and Sonnet reach a million tokens), but “huge” isn't “infinite,” and a cluttered window still hurts. The more irrelevant material is in view, the harder it is for the model to focus on the part that matters. A context window ","keywords":["think","of","it","as","ram","context"]},{"url":"code/context.html#tools","page":"Context is currency — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Four moves to keep it clean","tool":"code","text":"02 Four moves to keep it clean /clear Wipe the conversation and start fresh. Use it between unrelated tasks — the #1 habit. The old session stays in /resume . /compact Summarize the conversation so far, keeping key decisions and file state while reclaiming space. Add focus instructions: /compact kee","keywords":["four","moves","to","keep","it","clean","resume","compact","the","API","changes","undo","checkpoint"]},{"url":"code/context.html#habits","page":"Context is currency — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Two habits that pay off daily","tool":"code","text":"03 Two habits that pay off daily Clear between tasks The “kitchen-sink session” — one task, then an unrelated question, then back — pollutes context. Finish a thing, /clear , start the next clean. Scope your exploration “Investigate the whole codebase” reads hundreds of files and floods the window. ","keywords":["two","habits","that","pay","off","daily","clear","context","compact"]},{"url":"code/context.html#rewind","page":"Context is currency — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Checkpoints make experimenting safe","tool":"code","text":"04 Checkpoints make experimenting safe Every message is an automatic checkpoint of both the conversation and your files. So you can let Claude try a bold approach knowing you can undo it: Restore conversation only — keep the code, drop a confusing exchange. Restore code only — keep the discussion, t","keywords":["checkpoints","make","experimenting","safe"]},{"url":"code/explore-plan-code-commit.html","page":"Explore → Plan → Code → Commit — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Explore → Plan → Code → Commit","tool":"code","text":"Explore → Plan → Code → Commit This is the workflow that turns Claude Code from a slot machine into a reliable engineer. The idea is simple: separate understanding from doing. Let Claude explore and propose a plan before it writes a line — so you catch a wrong approach in a paragraph, not in a 300-l","keywords":["explore","plan","code","commit"]},{"url":"code/explore-plan-code-commit.html#four","page":"Explore → Plan → Code → Commit — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"The four phases","tool":"code","text":"01 The four phases The core loop 1 · explore read & ask no edits yet 2 · plan propose & approve you review 3 · code implement against the plan 4 · commit tests + git ship it Phases 1–2 happen in plan mode (read-only). You approve the plan, then switch to implementing. Measure twice, cut once analogy","keywords":["the","four","phases"]},{"url":"code/explore-plan-code-commit.html#plan-mode","page":"Explore → Plan → Code → Commit — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Plan mode, the safety rail","tool":"code","text":"02 Plan mode, the safety rail In plan mode , Claude can read and think but can't change files . It explores, then presents a plan for your approval. Enter it with /plan , or cycle permission modes with Shift + Tab . You can edit the plan before accepting it. a planning session Copy > /plan add rate ","keywords":["plan","mode","the","safety","rail","add","rate","limiting","to","public","API","Claude","reads","routes","middleware","config","then","proposes","You","read","it","tweak","and","approve","Only","THEN","does","write","code"]},{"url":"code/explore-plan-code-commit.html#watch","page":"Explore → Plan → Code → Commit — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Watch the whole loop","tool":"code","text":"03 Watch the whole loop Notice the mode pill in the corner switch to plan mode while Claude explores and proposes — then back to default once you approve and it starts editing. claude — ~/shop-api default An animated, step-through Claude Code session. Enable JavaScript to play it. ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5×","keywords":["watch","the","whole","loop"]},{"url":"code/explore-plan-code-commit.html#skip","page":"Explore → Plan → Code → Commit — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"When to skip planning","tool":"code","text":"04 When to skip planning Planning is for changes where the approach is uncertain. For tiny, obvious edits it's overhead. Plan it New features, anything touching multiple files, unfamiliar code, “I'm not sure how we'd do this,” or risky areas (auth, payments, migrations). Just do it Typos, a log line","keywords":["when","to","skip","planning"]},{"url":"code/verify.html","page":"Closing the loop — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Closing the loop","tool":"code","text":"Closing the loop Claude Code stops when the work looks done. Without a check it can run, you become the verification loop — every mistake waits for you to notice. The fix is to hand Claude a way to check itself: a test, a build, an example, a goal. That one habit is the difference between “probably ","keywords":["closing","the","loop"]},{"url":"code/verify.html#why","page":"Closing the loop — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"“Looks done” ≠ “is done”","tool":"code","text":"01 “Looks done” ≠ “is done” A model is very good at producing plausible code. Plausible isn't the same as correct. A check it can run — and read the result of — is what catches the gap. claude — ~/billing default An animated session showing the verify loop. Enable JavaScript to play it. ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / ","keywords":["looks","done","is"]},{"url":"code/verify.html#how","page":"Closing the loop — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Five ways to give Claude a check","tool":"code","text":"02 Five ways to give Claude a check In the prompt The simplest: “…and run the tests and fix failures,” or give example inputs/outputs. Claude iterates in the same turn. /goal Set a condition and Claude works across turns until it holds — great for unattended runs: /goal all tests pass . Stop hook A ","keywords":["five","ways","to","give","claude","check","goal","all","tests","pass","fix"]},{"url":"code/verify.html#root","page":"Closing the loop — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Insist on root causes","tool":"code","text":"03 Insist on root causes When a check fails, the lazy fix is to silence it — widen a type, wrap in a try/except, delete the assertion. Don't accept that. A good prompt says so: Watch for symptom-suppression gotcha If a test goes green because the test got weaker, that's worse than red. Tell Claude “","keywords":["insist","on","root","causes"]},{"url":"code/claude-md.html","page":"Project memory · CLAUDE.md — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Project memory · CLAUDE.md","tool":"code","text":"Project memory · CLAUDE.md CLAUDE.md is a short markdown file Claude Code reads at the start of every session. It's where you write the handful of things Claude can't guess from the code: how to build and test, conventions to follow, and the shape of the project. Think of it as the one-page onboardi","keywords":["project","memory","claude","md"]},{"url":"code/claude-md.html#where","page":"Project memory · CLAUDE.md — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Where it lives","tool":"code","text":"01 Where it lives ./CLAUDE.md Project root, checked into git — shared with your team. The main one. ./CLAUDE.local.md Project root, gitignored — your personal notes for this repo. ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md Your home directory — applies to all your projects (personal defaults). nested dirs A CLAUDE.md in a","keywords":["where","it","lives","CLAUDE","md","init","memory"]},{"url":"code/claude-md.html#what","page":"Project memory · CLAUDE.md — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"What to put in it","tool":"code","text":"02 What to put in it Keep it short and high-signal . Facts that change rarely and that aren't obvious from the code: CLAUDE.md Copy # Commands - Install: `uv sync` - Test (fast): `pytest -q` — prefer single tests during dev - Lint/format: `ruff check --fix && ruff format` # Conventions - Python 3.13","keywords":["what","to","put","in","it","Commands","Install","uv","sync","Test","fast","pytest","prefer","single","tests","during","dev","Lint","format","ruff","check","fix","Conventions","Python","type","hints","everywhere","list","int","None","Pydantic","v2","for","models","never","edit","files","generated","Architecture","API"]},{"url":"code/claude-md.html#not","page":"Project memory · CLAUDE.md — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"What to leave out","tool":"code","text":"03 What to leave out Don't restate the code gotcha Skip anything Claude can learn by reading: a file-by-file tour, standard language conventions, full API docs (link them), or details that change often. If Claude can find it in the repo, it doesn't belong in the memo.","keywords":["what","to","leave","out"]},{"url":"code/claude-md.html#auto","page":"Project memory · CLAUDE.md — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Auto-memory does some of this for you","tool":"code","text":"04 Auto-memory does some of this for you Claude Code also keeps an auto-memory : as it works, it quietly saves durable learnings — the real build command, a recurring gotcha, where a thing lives — so it doesn't rediscover them every session. Review or toggle it with /memory . Your hand-written CLAUD","keywords":["auto","memory","does","some","of","this","for","you","CLAUDE","md"]},{"url":"code/commands.html","page":"Slash commands — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Slash commands","tool":"code","text":"Slash commands Type / at the start of a message to run a command — a quick action like clearing context, switching models, or kicking off a review. They're the keyboard shortcuts of Claude Code. Type / alone to see every command available to you.","keywords":["slash","commands"]},{"url":"code/commands.html#essentials","page":"Slash commands — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"The dozen you'll actually use","tool":"code","text":"01 The dozen you'll actually use You don't need to memorize them all. These cover ~90% of day-to-day use: Manage the session /clear · /compact · /context · /rewind · /resume — keep the window clean and undo mistakes. Steer the model /plan · /model · /effort — choose how it works and how hard it thin","keywords":["the","dozen","you","ll","actually","use","clear","compact","context","rewind","resume","plan","model","effort","init","memory","permissions","mcp","agents","code","review","security"]},{"url":"code/commands.html#explorer","page":"Slash commands — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Explore every command","tool":"code","text":"02 Explore every command Filter and search the built-ins below. Click any row for details and an example. (The full reference — including settings, flags, hooks and env vars — lives on the cheat sheet .)","keywords":["explore","every","command"]},{"url":"code/commands.html#custom","page":"Slash commands — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Custom commands are just skills","tool":"code","text":"03 Custom commands are just skills Your own commands are now skills . Drop a markdown file in .claude/skills/ (or the older .claude/commands/ ) and it becomes a /command . The body is the instructions Claude follows; $ARGUMENTS receives whatever you type after the name. .claude/skills/fix-issue/SKIL","keywords":["custom","commands","are","just","skills","claude","command","ARGUMENTS","name","fix","issue","description","Investigate","and","GitHub","then","open","PR","Fix","gh","view","to","read","it","Find","the","relevant","files","understand","bug","Implement","add","test","Run","tests","linter","Commit","mcp__","server","__"]},{"url":"code/permissions.html","page":"Permissions & settings.json — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Permissions & settings.json","tool":"code","text":"Permissions & settings.json Claude Code is cautious by default: it asks before editing files or running commands. That's a safety feature, not a nuisance — and you can tune exactly how much it asks. Permissions are the guardrails; settings.json is where you set them.","keywords":["permissions","settings","json"]},{"url":"code/permissions.html#modes","page":"Permissions & settings.json — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Permission modes","tool":"code","text":"01 Permission modes The mode sets the default posture. Cycle modes with Shift + Tab , or start in one with --permission-mode . default Ask before each new action. Safest; a little chatty. Great while you're learning a project. plan Read-only — explore and propose, never edit. The research half of ex","keywords":["permission","modes","mode","dangerously","skip","permissions"]},{"url":"code/permissions.html#rules","page":"Permissions & settings.json — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Allow / ask / deny rules","tool":"code","text":"02 Allow / ask / deny rules For fine control, write rules in settings.json . allow runs without a prompt, ask always prompts, deny blocks outright. Patterns match tools and arguments. .claude/settings.json Copy { \"permissions\": { \"allow\": [ \"Bash(npm run test:*)\", \"Bash(git commit:*)\", \"Read\", \"Edit","keywords":["allow","ask","deny","rules","settings","json","permissions","Bash","npm","run","test","git","commit","Read","Edit","src","push","env","infra","defaultMode","acceptEdits","fewer","permission","prompts"]},{"url":"code/permissions.html#scopes","page":"Permissions & settings.json — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Where settings live","tool":"code","text":"03 Where settings live ~/.claude/settings.json User scope — your defaults across all projects. .claude/settings.json Project scope — checked into git, shared with the team. .claude/settings.local.json Local scope — personal, gitignored overrides for this repo. settings.json also holds env , hooks , ","keywords":["where","settings","live","json","env","hooks","model","statusLine","outputStyle"]},{"url":"code/permissions.html#philosophy","page":"Permissions & settings.json — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Match guardrails to trust","tool":"code","text":"04 Match guardrails to trust Like keys for a new hire analogy Day one, a new teammate gets read access and asks before touching production. As trust builds, you hand over more keys. Permissions work the same way: start in default , allowlist the safe-and-frequent, and reserve bypass for sandboxes yo","keywords":["match","guardrails","to","trust"]},{"url":"code/subagents.html","page":"Subagents — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Subagents","tool":"code","text":"Subagents A subagent is a second Claude with its own fresh context window that you delegate a task to. It does the work, then reports back a summary — keeping all that exploration out of your main conversation. Three jobs they're perfect for: investigate , verify , and parallelize .","keywords":["subagents"]},{"url":"code/subagents.html#why","page":"Subagents — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Why a separate context helps","tool":"code","text":"01 Why a separate context helps Reading twenty files to answer “where does auth live?” would flood your main window with code you don't need afterward. A subagent reads those files in its window and hands you back two sentences. Your context stays lean; you keep the conclusion, not the file dump. De","keywords":["why","separate","context","helps"]},{"url":"code/subagents.html#use","page":"Subagents — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Just ask for one","tool":"code","text":"02 Just ask for one You don't configure anything to start — describe the delegation in plain English: terminal Copy # investigate > use a subagent to find how token refresh works and report back # verify (a fresh, unbiased reviewer) > use a subagent to review this diff for edge cases and security is","keywords":["just","ask","for","one","investigate","use","subagent","to","find","how","token","refresh","works","and","report","back","verify","fresh","unbiased","reviewer","review","this","diff","edge","cases","security","issues","parallelize","independent","work","subagents","migrate","these","services","the","new","logging","API"]},{"url":"code/subagents.html#custom","page":"Subagents — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Custom subagents","tool":"code","text":"03 Custom subagents For roles you use often, define a subagent in .claude/agents/ . Give it a focused job, a limited tool set, and even a specific model. Manage them with /agents . .claude/agents/security-reviewer.md Copy --- name: security-reviewer description: Reviews diffs for security issues too","keywords":["custom","subagents","claude","agents","name","security","reviewer","description","Reviews","diffs","for","issues","tools","Read","Grep","Glob","Bash","model","opus","You","are","senior","engineer","Review","changes","injection","auth","authorization","flaws","secrets","in","code","and","unsafe","data","handling","Cite","specific","lines","suggest"]},{"url":"code/hooks.html","page":"Hooks — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Hooks","tool":"code","text":"Hooks A hook is a script that runs automatically at a specific moment — before a tool runs, after an edit, when a turn ends. The key difference from CLAUDE.md : a memo is advice Claude usually follows; a hook is code that always runs. When something must happen every single time, you want a hook.","keywords":["hooks"]},{"url":"code/hooks.html#advice-vs-guarantee","page":"Hooks — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Advice vs. guarantee","tool":"code","text":"01 Advice vs. guarantee A sign vs. a turnstile analogy “Please format your code” is a sign on the wall — usually heeded, sometimes missed. A PostToolUse hook that runs the formatter is a turnstile: every edit goes through it, no exceptions. Use memos for preferences, hooks for must-happens.","keywords":["advice","vs","guarantee","PostToolUse"]},{"url":"code/hooks.html#events","page":"Hooks — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"The events you'll use most","tool":"code","text":"02 The events you'll use most SessionStart A session begins — inject context, check the environment, warm a cache. UserPromptSubmit You hit enter — add context to the prompt or validate it before Claude sees it. PreToolUse Before a tool runs — can block it . Veto risky commands or writes to protecte","keywords":["the","events","you","ll","use","most"]},{"url":"code/hooks.html#config","page":"Hooks — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Wiring one up","tool":"code","text":"03 Wiring one up Hooks live in settings.json under hooks . A matcher selects which tools/events it applies to; the handler is usually a shell command (it also supports http, prompt, and agent handlers). .claude/settings.json Copy { \"hooks\": { \"PostToolUse\": [ { \"matcher\": \"Edit|Write\", \"hooks\": [{ \"","keywords":["wiring","one","up","settings","json","hooks","matcher","PostToolUse","Edit","Write","type","command","npx","prettier","write","FILE","PreToolUse","Bash","claude","guard","sh","Stop","npm","test"]},{"url":"code/hooks.html#uses","page":"Hooks — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Great uses","tool":"code","text":"04 Great uses Auto-format & lint PostToolUse on edits → run your formatter. Code stays clean without anyone remembering. Protect paths PreToolUse that blocks writes to migrations/ , infra/ , or generated files. Verify on stop Stop hook that runs tests and won't let the turn end red — see Closing the","keywords":["great","uses","PostToolUse","PreToolUse","migrations","infra","Stop"]},{"url":"code/skills-plugins.html","page":"Skills & plugins — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Skills & plugins","tool":"code","text":"Skills & plugins A skill is a folder with a SKILL.md that teaches Claude something — a convention, a checklist, a multi-step procedure. The clever part: a skill's body loads only when it's relevant , so you can keep lots of reference material around for almost no context cost. Plugins bundle skills ","keywords":["skills","plugins"]},{"url":"code/skills-plugins.html#what","page":"Skills & plugins — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Skills = knowledge on demand","tool":"code","text":"01 Skills = knowledge on demand Where CLAUDE.md is always loaded (so it must stay short), a skill loads lazily. Claude reads only the skill's one-line description until it's actually needed — then it pulls in the full body. That's why a procedure belongs in a skill, not the memo. .claude/skills/api-","keywords":["skills","knowledge","on","demand","CLAUDE","md","name","api","conventions","description","REST","for","our","services","use","when","adding","endpoints","API","Paths","kebab","case","JSON","fields","camelCase","Every","list","endpoint","is","paginated","limit","cursor","Version","in","the","path","v1","Errors","problem","json"]},{"url":"code/skills-plugins.html#invoke","page":"Skills & plugins — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Two kinds of skills","tool":"code","text":"02 Two kinds of skills Knowledge skills Facts and conventions Claude pulls in when relevant — like the API rules above. Auto-invoked. Workflow skills A procedure you trigger by name, like the /fix-issue command from the commands lesson. Often set disable-model-invocation: true so only you start them","keywords":["two","kinds","of","skills","fix","issue","disable","model","invocation","true","code","review","security","debug","run","verify"]},{"url":"code/skills-plugins.html#plugins","page":"Skills & plugins — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Plugins = bundles you can install","tool":"code","text":"03 Plugins = bundles you can install A plugin packages skills, subagents, hooks, MCP servers, and commands together. Install one and you get the whole kit — no copy-pasting config. terminal Copy # browse and install from a marketplace > /plugin # or from the shell claude plugin install code-review@c","keywords":["plugins","bundles","you","can","install","browse","and","from","marketplace","plugin","or","the","shell","claude","code","review","official","add","GitHub","repo","of","anthropics","community"]},{"url":"code/mcp.html","page":"MCP servers — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"MCP servers","tool":"code","text":"MCP servers MCP — the Model Context Protocol — is an open standard for connecting AI agents to outside systems: GitHub, your database, Sentry, Figma, Slack, anything. An MCP server exposes one of those systems as tools Claude can call. Connect one and Claude Code can use it natively — no copy-pastin","keywords":["mcp","servers"]},{"url":"code/mcp.html#apps","page":"MCP servers — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Like installing apps","tool":"code","text":"01 Like installing apps Out of the box, Claude Code can read files and run commands. MCP servers give it new capabilities — query Postgres, open a GitHub PR, read a Sentry issue — each one a small adapter speaking a shared protocol. One protocol, many tools Claude Code the agent speaks MCP MCP serve","keywords":["like","installing","apps"]},{"url":"code/mcp.html#add","page":"MCP servers — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Adding a server","tool":"code","text":"02 Adding a server Use claude mcp add with a transport: http for remote servers (most common), stdio for local processes. Then run /mcp to complete any OAuth login. terminal Copy # a remote (HTTP) server — e.g. GitHub claude mcp add --transport http github https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/ \\ --head","keywords":["adding","server","claude","mcp","add","remote","HTTP","GitHub","transport","http","github","https","api","githubcopilot","com","header","Authorization","Bearer","GITHUB_PAT","local","stdio","Postgres","bridge","db","npx","bytebase","dbhub","dsn","postgresql","user","pass","localhost","app","manage","them","list","authenticate","view","tool","counts"]},{"url":"code/mcp.html#scopes","page":"MCP servers — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Scopes & what you can do","tool":"code","text":"03 Scopes & what you can do local Just you, just this project (the default). project Shared with the team via a checked-in .mcp.json . user You, across all your projects. Once connected, you just ask in plain English and Claude uses the right tools: “Implement the feature in JIRA ENG-4521 and open a","keywords":["scopes","what","you","can","do","mcp","json"]},{"url":"code/power-user.html","page":"Power-user workflows — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Power-user workflows","tool":"code","text":"Power-user workflows You've got the everyday loop down. This lesson is the map of what's beyond a single session: running many Claudes at once, leaving work to run in the background, orchestrating fleets of agents, and — when you outgrow the CLI — building your own agents on the same engine.","keywords":["power","user","workflows"]},{"url":"code/power-user.html#parallel","page":"Power-user workflows — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Many sessions at once","tool":"code","text":"01 Many sessions at once Git worktrees claude -w feature-auth starts in an isolated checkout, so two sessions can work on two branches without stepping on each other. Background agents claude --bg \"fix the flaky test\" runs detached; check on all of them with claude agents or /tasks . From soloist to","keywords":["many","sessions","at","once","claude","feature","auth","bg","fix","the","flaky","test","agents","tasks"]},{"url":"code/power-user.html#orchestration","page":"Power-user workflows — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Orchestrating fleets","tool":"code","text":"02 Orchestrating fleets For changes that sprawl across a codebase, Claude Code can coordinate many agents automatically: ultracode Put the keyword in your prompt (or /effort ultracode ) and Claude spins up a team lead that decomposes the work and runs subtasks in parallel. Watch it with /workflows .","keywords":["orchestrating","fleets","effort","ultracode","workflows"]},{"url":"code/power-user.html#headless","page":"Power-user workflows — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Headless & scripted","tool":"code","text":"03 Headless & scripted claude -p runs a single query and exits — the bridge from interactive use to automation. Pipe data in, get an answer out, wire it into CI or a git hook. terminal Copy # answer one question and quit git diff | claude -p \"write a conventional-commit message for this diff\" # mach","keywords":["headless","scripted","claude","answer","one","question","and","quit","git","diff","write","conventional","commit","message","for","this","machine","readable","output","scripts","list","the","public","API","routes","format","json"]},{"url":"code/power-user.html#sdk","page":"Power-user workflows — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"Build your own agents","tool":"code","text":"04 Build your own agents When you want Claude Code's powers inside your own program — custom tools, your own permission logic, your own loop — reach for the Claude Agent SDK (Python & TypeScript). It's the same engine, exposed as a library. Continue in Track A tip The Agent SDK builds on the platfor","keywords":["build","your","own","agents"]},{"url":"code/power-user.html#wrap","page":"Power-user workflows — Claude Code · Field Guide","section":"You've finished Track B 🎉","tool":"code","text":"05 You've finished Track B 🎉 That's Claude Code, end to end — from your first session to orchestrating agents. The throughline never changed: gather context, act, verify, repeat , with you steering. Everything else is a refinement of that loop. Next, deepen the half that makes all of this better: h","keywords":["you","ve","finished","track"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html","page":"Cheat sheet & explorer — Reference · Field Guide","section":"Cheat sheet & explorer","tool":"reference","text":"Cheat sheet & explorer Everything in one place: the models, the core workflow, the shortcuts worth memorizing — and a searchable explorer of every command, setting, flag, hook and env var. Press / any time to jump elsewhere in the guide.","keywords":["cheat","sheet","explorer"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#models","page":"Cheat sheet & explorer — Reference · Field Guide","section":"Models (Jun 2026)","tool":"reference","text":"01 Models (Jun 2026) Model ID Use for Opus 4.8 claude-opus-4-8 Hardest reasoning, long agentic work Sonnet 4.6 claude-sonnet-4-6 The balanced default Haiku 4.5 claude-haiku-4-5 Fast, cheap, high-volume","keywords":["models","jun"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#workflow","page":"Cheat sheet & explorer — Reference · Field Guide","section":"The core loop & shortcuts","tool":"reference","text":"02 The core loop & shortcuts Explore → Plan → Code → Commit Explore in plan mode (read-only) · approve the plan · implement · give it a check to verify · commit. Skip the plan only if you could describe the diff in one sentence. Keys worth memorizing Esc interrupt · Esc ×2 rewind · Shift + Tab cycle","keywords":["the","core","loop","shortcuts","goal","Stop","code","review"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#explorer","page":"Cheat sheet & explorer — Reference · Field Guide","section":"The explorer","tool":"reference","text":"03 The explorer Filter by kind, type to search, click a row for detail and an example. Verified against the official docs, June 2026. This is a curated slice — Claude Code has many more commands and settings. The source of truth is always the official docs at code.claude.com/docs ; see the resources","keywords":["the","explorer","code","claude","com","docs"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html","page":"Glossary — Reference · Field Guide","section":"Glossary","tool":"reference","text":"Glossary Every term this guide leans on, in one plain-English line. Skim it, or / -search for the one that's tripping you up.","keywords":["glossary"]},{"url":"reference/resources.html","page":"Best GitHub references — Reference · Field Guide","section":"Best GitHub references","tool":"reference","text":"Best GitHub references Where to go deeper, hand-picked. Start with the official repos and docs — they're authoritative and current. The community lists are great for discovering patterns and extensions once you know the basics.","keywords":["best","github","references"]},{"url":"reference/resources.html#end","page":"Best GitHub references — Reference · Field Guide","section":"end","tool":"reference","text":"That's the guide tip You've got the model and the tool, end to end. The fastest way to cement it: open a real project, run claude , and put the explore→plan→code→commit loop to work. Come back to the cheat sheet whenever you need it.","keywords":["end","claude"]}];
